     4f      Yb      13       5.878      8.909   -0.11140 Ha    0.16664       0.668

     SIX OF SEVEN CORRIDOR ROWS SIT WITHIN 0.018 Ha, and so does every zero-sibling opening.
     YTTERBIUM ALONE IS 0.111 Ha -- six times the next largest.  The undelivered fraction runs from
     0.018 at xenon to 1.049 at gallium with no order in sibling count, sibling energy or <r>, so
     there is no measured law that converts a sibling count into a correction, and a transfer
     built on ytterbium's fraction alone has no support.  What the ladder gives is a BOUND, and
     the bound is what a one-sibling row is entitled to: |residual| <= 0.018 Ha, and inside the
     zero-sibling band of +-0.004 Ha at two of the three one-sibling rows.

     Gallium is the ladder's worst row at -0.0174 Ha and has a named reason: its 4s sits directly
     above a filled 3d10, and the 4s removal carries a d-shell relaxation none of the others do.

